{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Blessed be the fruit of your body, etc.</b> And afterward it says: ״בָּרוּךְ טַנְאֲךָ וּמִשְׁאַרְתֶּךָ״, but in the curses it reversed the order, mentioning טַנְאֲךָ וּמִשְׁאַרְתֶּךָ before פְּרִי בִטְנְךָ. For reason dictates that the Holy One, blessed be He, will always perform a hidden miracle more than He will perform a revealed miracle. And the blessing of the fruit of the body and the fruit of the ground are all hidden miracles — even if they multiply beyond the usual, this is not outside nature. But טַנְאֲךָ וּמִשְׁאַרְתֶּךָ, which are detached fruits already placed in a vessel — if they multiply, this is a revealed miracle, whether one puts down two and finds three, in the manner stated (1 Kings 17:14): ״כַּד הַקֶּמַח לֹא תִכְלָה״ etc., or whether one eats a morsel and is blessed in his innards — all of it is a revealed miracle. Therefore in the blessings it says: not only a hidden miracle will He perform for you, but even a revealed miracle will He perform for you. And in the curses it is the reverse: not only this but even that." "And as for what is said immediately after ״בָּרוּךְ אַתָּה בְּצֵאתְךָ״ — ״יִתֵּן ה׳ אֶת אֹיְבֶיךָ נִגָּפִים לְפָנֶיךָ״ — whereas in the curses it does not mention immediately that they will be defeated, but only after several verses: this is because reason dictates that immediately, when the enemies see that all the inhabitants of that land are successful and blessed in their coming and in their going, then the nations will be envious of Israel and will go forth from their homes to wage war against Israel to drive them from that good land. And concerning this it says: ״בְּדֶרֶךְ אֶחָד יֵצְאוּ אֵלֶיךָ״ etc. — meaning that the nations will come against Israel. But in the curses it says: ״בְּדֶרֶךְ אֶחָד תֵּצֵא אֵלָיו״ — meaning, on the contrary, that Israel will go forth from their land to wage war against the nations. And this is because in the curses, the nations that saw the plagues of the land and its diseases — presumably they would not seek the land of Israel, for they would see in it no success whatsoever. Therefore it was not said after ״אָרוּר אַתָּה בְּצֵאתְךָ״: ״יִתֶּנְךָ ה׳ נִגָּף לִפְנֵי אוֹיְבֶיךָ״, with enemies that would come against you — for surely they would not come; rather, you would go forth from your land toward them, for you would see that their land is better than yours. But not immediately; rather, only after all the evil prophecies He mentioned are fulfilled, from ״יְשַׁלַּח ה׳ בְּךָ אֶת הַמְּאֵרָה״ until ״יִתֵּן ה׳ מְטַר אַרְצְךָ אָבָק וְעָפָר״ etc., ״וְהָיוּ שָׁמֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר עַל רֹאשְׁךָ נְחֹשֶׁת, וְהָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר תַּחְתֶּיךָ בַּרְזֶל״. עַל רֹאשְׁךָ וְתַחְתֶּיךָ — why do I need this? Do we not yet know that the heavens are above your head and the earth beneath you? Rather, He wished to say: that particular part that is above your head and beneath you will be ruined — but the heavens and the earth above and below, belonging to the lands of the other peoples, will not be ruined. And specifically מְטַר אַרְצְךָ — the rain of your land — not the rain of the lands of the other nations. And therefore your heart will prompt you to go forth from your home to the lands of the other nations that are better than yours — perhaps you will be able to drive them out to settle in their place. Therefore it says: ״יִתֶּנְךָ ה׳ נִגָּף לִפְנֵי אֹיְבֶיךָ, בְּדֶרֶךְ אֶחָד תֵּצֵא אֵלָיו וּבְשִׁבְעָה דְרָכִים תָּנוּס לְפָנָיו״. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:24.381650943Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}